# For the dewey.ps1 / dewey.sh install. (With Docker Compose these same keys go in .env instead: see .env.example.)
# Copy this file to web.env ON THE NAS, next to dewey.sh (not on the PC), then run:  .\dewey.ps1 web
# One KEY=value per line, no quotes. Use a long random password (generate it in your password manager).
MD_WEB_USER=admin
MD_WEB_PASS=change-this-password
# One login per person: add pairs numbered _2 to _9 (each needs both lines). The History then shows who started each job.
# MD_WEB_USER_2=guest
# MD_WEB_PASS_2=another-long-password

# --- security (see Settings > Security in the console) ---------------------------------------------------------
# Publish the port on the NAS itself only. Do this AFTER the https address works (Tailscale Serve, see README "Security"):
# plain http://<nas>:8099 then stops answering for every other device.
# MD_WEB_BIND=127.0.0.1
#
# Only answer to these host names (comma separated). The Security panel shows the exact name your browser used.
# MD_WEB_HOSTS=nas.your-tailnet.ts.net
#
# MD_WEB_PORT=8099

# --- Homepage (or another dashboard) tile ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A token that may read GET /api/widget (about twenty counts and one timestamp, nothing else). Make one on the NAS with:
#   head -c 24 /dev/urandom | od -An -tx1 | tr -d ' \n'
# MD_WIDGET_TOKEN=
# Also join this docker network so the dashboard container can reach http://dewey-web:8099/api/widget without any port open
# on the LAN. It is the network Homepage runs on (find it: sudo docker inspect homepage --format '{{range $k,$v := .NetworkSettings.Networks}}{{$k}} {{end}}'):
# MD_WEB_NETWORK=your_dashboard_network

# --- real runs (the runner; see README "Phase 2") ---------------------------------------------------------------
# How long arming lasts, in minutes (1 to 120). Arming needs a code from your authenticator app, checked inside the runner.
# MD_ARM_MINUTES=15
